<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.8.2: Represent a Function as a Table of Values, Graph, and Equation</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01386</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.8.2: Represent a Function as a Table of Values, Graph, and Equation</md:title>
    <md:uuid>cfe85e68-db35-4ffe-8d4d-56ad5e60f633</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Clare noticed mold on the last slice of bread in a plastic bag. The area covered by the mold was about 1 square
  millimeter. She left the bread alone to see how the mold would grow. The next day, the area covered by the mold had
  doubled, and it doubled again the day after that.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> If the doubling pattern continues, how many square millimeters will the mold cover 4 days after she noticed
        the mold? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>16 square millimeters. \(2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2 \cdot 2\) or \(2^4\) is 16.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> Represent the relationship between the area \(A\), in square millimeters, covered by the mold and the number
        of days \(d\) since the mold was spotted using:
        <para> A table of values, showing the values from the day the mold was spotted through 5 days later. </para>
          <para> An equation </para>
          <para> A graph </para>
        </para>
    <para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answers:</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item/>
    <table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
      <thead>
        <row>
          <entry><emphasis>Number of days, \(d\)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Area, \(A\), in sq mm</emphasis></entry></row>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <row>
          <entry>
            0
          </entry>
          <entry>
           1
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
        1
          </entry>
          <entry>
            2
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
           2
          </entry>
          <entry>
            4
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
           3
          </entry>
          <entry>
           8
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
          4
          </entry>
          <entry>
          16
          </entry>
        </row>
        <row>
          <entry>
            5
          </entry>
          <entry>
           32
          </entry>
        </row>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    
    <item> Equation: \(A=1 \cdot 2^d\) or equivalent (like \(A=2^d\)). </item>
    
    <item>Your graph may be discrete or continuous, this example is a discrete graph.
      <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/12fcc0a764a3422882fd1d726e1ca3ad71b343f0"/></media></figure></item>
  </list>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Discuss with your partner: Is the relationship between the area covered by mold and the number of days a
        function? If so, write ____ is a function of ____. If not, explain why it is not.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>Yes. </para>
    <list>
      <item>The area covered by mold is a function of the number of days that have passed. At any given time since mold
        was spotted, there is a certain area of the bread that is covered in mold.</item>
      <item>The number of days that have passed is a function of the area covered by the mold up to a certain point (e.g.,
        when the entire piece of bread is already completely covered, the area of mold would correspond to more than one
        day). We do not have a way to write this relationship using an equation, but we could use a table or a graph to
        show the relationship.</item>
    </list>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


